WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.200
A neuron flashes brighter for a moment.

2
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.200
How many spikes did it fire?

3
00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:10.133
The glow is measured in light, not spikes.

4
00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:14.253
To translate, record both at once in a sample of neurons.

5
00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:17.613
Then ask whether that calibration fits your conditions.

6
00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:22.987
A fine glass pipette presses against the outside of the cell, without breaking in.

7
00:00:23.707 --> 00:00:25.367
It picks up each spike electrically.

8
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.830
This is a cell-attached recording.

9
00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.270
Meanwhile, the microscope films the same cell's glow.

10
00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:33.043
Picture the record.

11
00:00:34.063 --> 00:00:37.703
Spike ticks from the pipette run along the top, and the glow runs below.

12
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:39.993
The spikes are the answer key.

13
00:00:40.873 --> 00:00:42.653
You ask how often the light gets them right.

14
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:48.097
The detection rate is the fraction of isolated single spikes that the light reveals.

15
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.540
The false-positive rate is the fraction of spike-free test windows in which the

16
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:54.920
light reports a spike anyway.

17
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:59.290
You can always find more spikes by accepting more false alarms.

18
00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:04.330
So fair comparisons fix the false alarms at 1% % of spike-free windows.

19
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.360
First, the best case.

20
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.820
Chen and colleagues made detection as easy as possible, with a magnified view of one

21
00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:12.650
cell.

22
00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:17.190
The sensor, GCaMP6s, revealed nearly every isolated spike.

23
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:20.240
Now widen the field to hold hundreds of cells.

24
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.300
Each cell becomes a tiny part of the picture.

25
00:01:23.933 --> 00:01:26.493
What happens to single-spike detection, and why?

26
00:01:32.083 --> 00:01:37.603
It falls further. Huang and colleagues repeated the calibration in transgenic mice.

27
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:43.413
Even at high magnification, their tuned detector caught fewer single spikes than in

28
00:01:43.413 --> 00:01:48.853
Chen's cells. The authors proposed weaker expression and more labelled neuropil as

29
00:01:48.853 --> 00:01:49.853
possible reasons.

30
00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:54.187
When the movies were made to mimic a wide field, it caught only a minority.

31
00:01:54.967 --> 00:02:00.167
Standard software without the answer key often caught under one spike in ten, even

32
00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:01.167
before that.

33
00:02:01.317 --> 00:02:03.337
Huang and colleagues pointed to photons.

34
00:02:04.297 --> 00:02:07.437
A wider field spreads the imaging time over more pixels.

35
00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:10.287
Each neuron sends back fewer photons.

36
00:02:11.347 --> 00:02:15.267
Photon counts flicker at random, and one spike's glow sinks into the flicker.

37
00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:21.267
If the cell sends back the same photons per second, a slower frame rate costs a slow

38
00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:24.487
sensor little. Frame rate still limits spike timing.

39
00:02:25.117 --> 00:02:28.777
So a calibration belongs to its conditions, not to the sensor.

40
00:02:29.417 --> 00:02:34.077
Before turning glow into spikes, ask: was it calibrated at your field of view,

41
00:02:34.357 --> 00:02:35.817
expression level and cell type?

42
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.480
And in population imaging, a flat trace does not prove a silent cell.

43
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.960
One or a few spikes can hide in it.
